---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Section from '../../components/layout/Section.astro';
import Container from '../../components/layout/Container.astro';
import { FALLBACK_EMAIL } from '../../utils/forms/submit';
const mailto = 'mailto:' + FALLBACK_EMAIL;
const lastUpdated = '18 August 2026';
---
<PageLayout
  title="Editorial &amp; Trust Policy"
  description="How Marlbridge writes, verifies, versions and corrects the study material it publishes, and how to report an error."
  path="/legal/editorial-policy/"
  eyebrow="Legal"
  heading="Editorial &amp; Trust Policy"
  headingFont="sans"
  lead={`Last updated ${lastUpdated}.`}
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Editorial & Trust Policy', href: '/legal/editorial-policy/' }]}
>
  <Section surface="white">
    <Container width="prose">
      <div class="text-[17.5px] leading-[1.75] [&>h2]:mt-10 [&>h2]:text-[26px] [&>h2]:font-semibold [&>h2]:text-navy-800 [&>h3]:mt-8 [&>h3]:text-[20px] [&>h3]:font-semibold [&>p]:mt-5 [&>ul]:mt-5 [&>ul]:ps-5 [&>ul>li]:mt-2">
        <p class="m-0 border-s-2 border-gold-500 ps-4 text-[15.5px] text-ink-mute">
          This page sets out how Marlbridge's published study material is written, verified, versioned and
          corrected, and who is currently accountable for it. It describes the site as it actually operates
          today, including where that falls short of an ideal — it does not describe an aspiration.
        </p>

        <h2 id="editorial-policy">Editorial policy</h2>
        <p>
          Marlbridge publishes study guides, revision notes and practice material written against the specific,
          named examination-board syllabus each resource declares (see its "Syllabus" and board labels). Content
          is written by, or under the direction of, the Marlbridge Academic Team — a team, not a named individual,
          until real individual author profiles are introduced (see "Authorship" below). New content is added
          topic by topic as it is written; pages are not published as placeholders and then backfilled.
        </p>

        <h2 id="academic-review-policy">Academic review policy</h2>
        <p>
          Every resource is written against the official syllabus document for the board, qualification and
          syllabus code it declares, and is checked against that document before publication. Marlbridge does
          not currently operate a separate, named second-reviewer sign-off step distinct from the author — this
          is a genuine limitation, stated plainly rather than implied away with review-sounding language the
          site cannot back up. Introducing a distinct named-reviewer step is tracked as future work (see
          "Corrections" below for how errors are still caught and fixed in the meantime).
        </p>

        <h2 id="syllabus-verification-policy">Syllabus verification policy</h2>
        <p>
          Every syllabus summary and official-source citation on Marlbridge is checked directly against the
          awarding body's own current published specification and qualification pages — never against a
          tutoring site, a forum, a search-engine snippet, or general background knowledge. Each syllabus entry
          records the exact official URL used and the date it was checked (visible as "Verified" text on the
          relevant qualification page). Where a syllabus has not yet been checked this way, the page says so
          honestly ("being verified") rather than guessing at content.
        </p>

        <h3>Legacy and revised specifications</h3>
        <p>
          Where a board runs two versions of a specification side by side during a transition (an outgoing
          "legacy" code and an incoming "revised" code), Marlbridge records both codes, their exact first
          -teaching and final-exam dates, and which one currently applies to a new starter — this is called out
          explicitly wherever it applies, not merged into a single ambiguous code.
        </p>

        <h2 id="corrections-policy">Corrections policy</h2>
        <p>
          If you find a factual error, an outdated syllabus reference, or a broken citation anywhere on
          Marlbridge, please report it — this is the single most useful thing a reader can do for the site.
          Write to <a href={mailto} class="underline decoration-gold-500 underline-offset-2">{FALLBACK_EMAIL}</a>
          with the page URL and what looks wrong. Reports are read and, where the correction is confirmed against
          the official source, the page is updated and its "Verified" date is refreshed. Marlbridge does not
          currently publish a public changelog of individual corrections; the "Last updated"/"Verified" date on
          each page is the record of when it was last checked.
        </p>

        <h2 id="responsible-ai-disclosure">Responsible use of AI</h2>
        <p>
          Some of the drafting, editing, validation tooling and site engineering behind Marlbridge is produced
          with the assistance of AI systems, under human direction and review. Academic facts — syllabus codes,
          specification content, assessment structure — are always checked against the awarding body's own
          official source before publication, regardless of how a draft was produced; an AI system's own
          background knowledge is never treated as a citable source for an academic claim on this site. Original
          practice questions and worked examples are reviewed for correctness before publication in the same way
          as any other content.
        </p>

        <h2 id="content-versioning">Content versioning</h2>
        <p>
          Each resource and syllabus page carries a verification or "last updated" date rather than a formal
          version number. When an examination board revises a specification (a new syllabus code, a new
          examination series, or a legacy-to-revised transition), Marlbridge updates the affected pages to
          reflect the current specification and records the change in that page's date and, where relevant, in
          its stated applicability years. Superseded specification content is not silently deleted where a
          legacy code remains examinable — see "Legacy and revised specifications" above.
        </p>

        <h2 id="copyright-and-original-content">Copyright and original questions</h2>
        <p>
          Marlbridge does not reproduce awarding-body specification text, past-exam questions, or mark schemes.
          Syllabus summaries on this site are original explanations of what a specification covers, written from
          the official document rather than copied from it. Practice questions published on Marlbridge are
          wholly original, written by the Marlbridge Academic Team for the relevant syllabus — they are not
          drawn from, or a rewording of, any board's past papers. Where a resource discusses or links to a
          board's own past papers, it directs readers to the board's own official source rather than
          reproducing the material itself.
        </p>

        <h2 id="authorship">Authorship and accountability</h2>
        <p>
          Published work currently carries the <a href="/authors/marlbridge-academic-team/" class="underline decoration-gold-500 underline-offset-2">Marlbridge Academic Team</a> byline
          — a team, not an individual — because Marlbridge has not yet published real, named individual author
          profiles with genuine credentials. Individual profiles will be introduced, with real names and
          verifiable qualifications, rather than invented ones; nothing on this site attributes content to a
          named person who does not exist.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>
</PageLayout>
